# Scotland Panel — Methodology Notes

This note documents the decisions and diagnostics behind the Scotland
extension to the LA-level retail/social care panel, kept separate from
the main README so the England pipeline's documentation stays untouched.
Companion to the existing RETAIL_METHODOLOGY_NOTE.md pattern used
elsewhere in this project.

**Status as of this note:** specification settled and validated
(29-council sample, unweighted, F=26.63). `scotland_panel.py` built and
run successfully. Main IV results obtained (Section 7). Sub-sector
"reasons" descriptive finding added (Section 8).

---

## 1. Vacancy data source

**Chosen source:** "Staff vacancies in care services" — annual joint
report, Care Inspectorate and Scottish Social Services Council (SSSC).
Matches what `panel_data.py`'s docstring already anticipated.

**Explicitly NOT used:** the SSSC "Social worker filled posts and
vacancies six-monthly survey" — a different, narrower series covering
only local-authority-employed social workers, with incomplete coverage
each wave (23–31 of 32 councils respond, varies by round). Easy to
confuse with the correct source; do not substitute one for the other.

**Scope limitation (documented, not fixed):** the chosen report covers
**all social services** — children, young people, adults, and older
people combined (only childminders excluded). England's Skills for Care
data, by contrast, is **adult social care only**. This is a genuine,
unavoidable scope mismatch, not a methodological choice — the LA-level
tables in the SSSC report have no age-group breakdown at all (age-group
splits — e.g. "Care homes: Adults" vs "Care homes: Older people" — only
exist at *national* level; the LA-level cross-tabs split only by
ownership sector (Private/Public/Voluntary), never by age group). It is
structurally impossible to get an adult-only LA-level series from this
source. Decision: accept the all-ages scope for Scotland, document the
mismatch plainly wherever Scotland is compared to England in the paper.
Consequence: the "aging population" framing (`pop_65plus` as a demand
driver) is weaker for Scotland's outcome variable specifically, though
`pop_65plus` remains a legitimate control/regressor regardless.

**Usable table:** Table 1j ("Percentage of whole time equivalent (WTE)
vacancies as a proportion of the total WTE workforce by local authority
area") — gives `vacancy_rate` directly, LA name (not code), by year.

**No raw count equivalent available.** Table 1e gives "number of
services reporting a vacancy" (an establishment headcount), not a count
of vacant posts. There is no LA-level WTE vacancy *count* analogous to
England's `vacant_posts` in this report. Deriving one would require
sourcing total WTE workforce by LA separately (the SSSC's annual
Workforce Data Report, a different publication) and back-calculating
`vacancy_rate × total_workforce` — not yet done, decision pending.

**Year coverage — better than first assumed.** Initially thought limited
to 2021–2023 (one report vintage). Turns out the 2022-vintage report is
published as a PDF with its LA-level tables embedded directly in the
text (no separate spreadsheet before the 2023 vintage — confirmed only
2023 and 2024 reports have a "supporting tables" companion file; 2022
and earlier are PDF-only). Extracting Table 1j directly from the 2022
PDF's text gives 2020/2021/2022 without needing any further sourcing.

Combining all three available sources (2022 PDF, 2023 supporting
tables, 2024 supporting tables) gives **2020–2024, five years**, with
every overlapping year cross-checked byte-for-byte across vintages:

| Year | Source(s) | Cross-checked across vintages? |
|---|---|---|
| 2020 | 2022 PDF only | No (no other vintage covers it) |
| 2021 | 2022 PDF + 2023 file | Yes — exact match |
| 2022 | 2022 PDF + 2023 file + 2024 file | Yes — exact match across all three |
| 2023 | 2023 file + 2024 file | Yes — exact match |
| 2024 | 2024 file only | No (no other vintage covers it yet) |

No revisions detected between vintages for any spot-checked LA/year —
SSSC's published figures appear stable once released, so the "prefer
the most recent vintage" rule barely matters in practice, but is still
followed for principled consistency.

**Decision: use 2020–2023 (4 years) for this analysis**, per the
2016–2023 window decision — matches the pattern below more precisely
than the originally-assumed 3-year window. **2024 is available and
held in reserve for the separate extension thread**, not used here.

**Reaching further back (2016–2019)** would require sourcing the
2019/2020/2021-vintage report PDFs and manually extracting their
Table 1j the same way as done for 2022 (each is PDF-only, same rolling
3-year-window pattern). Confirmed feasible, not pursued now — explicitly
deferred, "they are there but not really needed for this set of
analyses."

**No sector split.** Per explicit decision: use the aggregate,
whole-sector `vacancy_rate` per LA per year. Do not attempt the kind of
service-type/job-role disaggregation Skills for Care allowed for
England.

