## 2. Retail chain classification

**Co-op already handled correctly** in `retail_panel.py`'s
`INCUMBENTS_SCOTLAND` set — all major regional Co-op societies (The
Co-operative Group, Scottish Midland Co-operative, The Southern
Co-operative, Central England Co-operative, Midcounties Co-operative,
East of England Co-operative, Lincolnshire Co-operative) are counted as
incumbents, with the documented rationale that Co-op has ~31.8% of
Scottish major retail footprint vs. ~22% in England. Waitrose is
correctly excluded for Scotland (only 6 stores — negligible) and
included for England. This was already built and documented before this
session; raised as a concern, checked, confirmed correct, no code
change needed.

---

## 3. Bartik instrument — Scotland-only construction

**Leave-one-out pool: Scotland-only (Option A), not pooled with
England.** Decided explicitly to avoid touching England's already-
validated instrument and results. `construct_bartik()` from
`bartik_check.py` is fully generic (only needs `la_code`/`year`/
`tot`/`tot_new`/`tot_old`) and requires no modification — feeding it a
Scotland-only dataframe automatically produces a Scotland-only
leave-one-out pool.

**No geographic density control in the first stage — deliberate,
not an oversight.** Considered whether retail chains growing in
geographic clusters (agglomeration effects) warranted a density control.
Rejected: density risks being a "bad control" — itself partly a
*consequence* of the expansion process being instrumented, rather than a
predetermined characteristic. Controlling for it would mean controlling
for part of the treatment itself, biasing the estimate. Left out of the
causal specification entirely; could only ever be examined as a
separate descriptive diagnostic, never folded into the main design.

**Finding: Scotland's 2016 baseline discount-share distribution is
structurally narrower than England's.**

| | England | Scotland (32 councils) |
|---|---|---|
| Mean | 0.161 | 0.128 |
| Median | 0.168 | 0.136 |
| Min | 0.000 | 0.000 |
| Max | 0.370 | 0.207 |

This is a real, structural difference in retail composition across
Scottish vs. English councils — not a sample-size artefact.

**First-stage diagnostic (`check_scotland_bartik.py`), 2020–2023:**

| Sample | F-statistic | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| All 32 councils | 7.52 | WEAK (<10) |
| 29 councils (3 islands excluded) | **26.63** | STRONG (>16.38 Stock-Yogo) |
| England, 148 LAs (reference) | 30.84 | STRONG |

**Island exclusion — decided on structural grounds, before seeing this
result.** Orkney Islands, Shetland Islands, and Na h-Eileanan Siar
(Western Isles) excluded on the same *kind* of justification as
England's City of London/Isles of Scilly exclusion: remote archipelago
geography, populations in the low 20-thousands — decided on what these
councils *are*, independent of how they performed in the growth data.
Excluding on the basis of observed weak growth instead would be
selecting on the outcome and would invalidate the exercise; this is
explicitly not that. The criterion was fixed before running the
diagnostic that confirmed its effect.

**Mechanism confirmed:** two of the three excluded islands had *exactly
zero* discount-chain (Aldi/Lidl) presence in the 2016 baseline —
Na h-Eileanan Siar (7 total stores, 0 discount) and Shetland Islands (3
total stores, 0 discount). A zero baseline share is not just an outlier;
the Bartik instrument cannot be meaningfully computed as a proportion
for a unit with no discount presence at all in the baseline year. Orkney
was not among the lowest-share councils (some discount presence
existed) but was excluded on the same structural (remote island) basis
as the other two, for consistency.

**Specification, as settled:**
- 29 councils (islands excluded from both instrument construction and
  regression sample)
- Unweighted (OLS/2SLS with cluster-robust SEs, same as England)
- 2016 baseline shares + Scotland-only leave-one-out growth
- No region/density controls beyond population (`pop_total`,
  `pop_65plus`)

---

## 4. Population weighting — considered and explicitly rejected

Raised as an alternative to island exclusion (down-weight small councils
rather than drop them). Not pursued, for three reasons:

1. **Different target quantity, not a fix.** Weighting by population
   shifts the estimate toward the effect experienced by the average
   *person* (Glasgow/Edinburgh-dominated) rather than the average
   *council* — a substantive change in what's being estimated, not a
   technical patch for weak identification.
2. **Uncertain direction of effect.** Whether weighting would even
   improve the F-stat depends on which councils anchor the low end of
   the baseline-share distribution; untested and not assumed.
3. **Unresolved technical complexity, never checked against a proper
   reference.** Combining population weights with cluster-robust
   standard errors requires adjusting the sandwich estimator's score
   contributions — not a simple bolt-on, and doing it from memory
   without checking a source (e.g. Cameron & Miller's clustering guide,
   or how `reghdfe`/`ivreg2` implement `aweight`+`cluster()` together)
   risks confidently-wrong SEs. Also raised: no clear way to interpret a
   second stage mixing a *rate* outcome (vacancy_rate) against a *count*
   outcome (benefit claimants) consistently under population weights.

Since island exclusion alone resolved the instrument-strength problem
(F=7.52 → 26.63) without any of this complexity, and keeps Scotland
methodologically identical to England's unweighted approach throughout,
weighting was dropped as unnecessary. If revisited later, do the
literature check first — do not improvise weighted-clustered SE code.

---

## 5. Window — deliberately held at 2016–2023

Explicit decision: publish the current England (2016–2023) analysis
first, since it underpins a PhD student's work that has already been
picked up by policymakers. Extending the window (England's 2024/25
Skills for Care refresh — confirmed to already exist, per the
"State of the adult social care sector... 2025" report showing full
2024/25 figures — plus Geolytix v47+, plus any further Scotland SSSC
report years) is real, current, and worth doing, but explicitly
**deferred to a separate thread**, kept apart from this validation/
publication effort so scope creep doesn't destabilize what's about to
be submitted.

---

## 6. Main IV results (`check_scotland_iv.py`), 2020-2023

29 councils, islands excluded, same `run_iv()` machinery as England.

| Outcome | Scotland | England (benchmark) |
|---|---|---|
| Vacancy rate | β=0.085, p=0.422 | β=0.131, p=0.257 (both null) |
| Benefit rate (Dec) | β=−0.052, p=0.846 | β=−1.057, p=0.001 |
| Benefit rate (annual) | β=0.086, p=0.648 | β=+0.632, p<0.001 |

First-stage F=26.63 (2020-2023 window specifically), same solid
instrument as the earlier diagnostic -- these are trustworthy nulls,
not artefacts of a broken first stage (unlike several of the weak/
degenerate results seen during today's regional-level England work).

**Interpretation:**
- **Vacancies replicate across both nations** -- retail expansion
  doesn't appear to hurt social care staffing capacity in either
  country. Genuine cross-national agreement, not just two separate
  nulls.
- **Benefits do NOT replicate.** England's benefit-claims effect is
  large and significant in both specifications; Scotland's point
  estimates are close to zero (not just imprecise -- the coefficient
  itself is ~20x smaller, e.g. -0.052 vs -1.057 for December), which
  looks like a genuine divergence rather than an underpowered version
  of England's finding.
- **Candidate explanation, tentative:** Scotland's retail incumbent
  base is dominated by regional Co-op societies (~31.8% of major
  retail footprint vs ~22% in England -- see Section 2) rather than a
  few large national chains. A structurally different competitive
  landscape could plausibly mean Scottish retail expansion doesn't
  displace workers into new roles the same way England's does. This
  is a hypothesis for the discussion section, not a proven mechanism.

---

## 7. Sub-sector descriptive evidence: "reasons vacancies are hard to
fill" by service type

Verified directly against the structured `Tables3e-3j` sheet (not the
garbled PDF-extracted text) in both the 2023 and 2024 supporting-tables
files. Table 3e gives "Competition from other types of work" -- the
category directly capturing loss of candidates to alternative
employment (e.g. retail) -- broken down by Care Inspectorate service
type, 2021-2023.

| Service type | Competition from other work (2023/2022/2021) | vs. national average |
|---|---|---|
| National average | 31% / 28% / 23% | -- |
| Care at home | 51% / 50% / 48% | Much higher |
| Housing support | 52% / 52% / 45% | Much higher |
| Care homes: Adults | 46% / 44% / 36% | Higher |
| Care homes: Older people | 39% / 36% / 31% | Higher |
| Nurse agency (skilled/higher-wage) | 21% / 26% / 19% | Below/near average |
| Fostering (professionally qualified) | 13% / 13% / 13% | Much lower |
| Daycare of children | 17% / 16% / 15% | Lower |

**Reading:** the lowest-paid, least-qualified roles -- care at home and
housing support specifically, arguably the closest in pay/skill level
to retail work -- report losing candidates to "other types of work" at
roughly double the sector average, rising each year. More skilled/
professional roles (nursing, fostering) show the opposite pattern. A
plausible, concrete sub-sector mechanism consistent with a retail-
competition story sitting underneath the aggregate null vacancy_rate
result in Section 7.

**Limitation -- worth being explicit about, unprompted:** this table is
**national-level by service type only**. Confirmed no LA-level cross-
tab exists for this breakdown, same structural gap as the vacancy_rate
tables (Section 1) -- SSSC's LA-level tables only cross with ownership
*sector* (private/public/voluntary), never with service *type* (age
group/role). So this cannot feed into the quantitative regression (no
way to build an LA-level "share of low-wage roles" variable from it) --
it stands as **descriptive/qualitative evidence for the discussion
section only**, not something the IV design can test directly. That
this table genuinely can't be crossed with geography is a real
constraint of the published data, not a choice.

