---

## 9. Closing reflection: identification vs. data granularity

Worth recording as the takeaway from this whole session, separate from
any specific result, because it's a paper-worthy point in its own
right, not just a project note.

Section 3 (LA level) has a strong, valid instrument (F=30.84, well
above Stock-Yogo). That's real internal validity -- the estimated
effect of retail expansion on care outcomes and benefit claims for
that specification is credible. But the moment the question shifts
from "what is the effect" to "does the effect depend on overseas
recruitment access" -- a *mechanism* question -- the only data that
exists to test it (PAYE nationality breakdown) is published at just 9
English regions. No amount of instrument validity at LA level carries
over to that question, because the mechanism variable itself doesn't
exist at LA level. Tried multiple reasonable specifications at
regional level today (binary split, continuous interaction, fixed
2016 baseline, contemporaneous) -- every one returns an honest null or
an unidentifiable estimate, not because the design is wrong, but
because 9 regions cannot support the level of precision the underlying
policy question actually requires.

**The general point, worth a paper section:** having a valid instrument
answers "is this LATE credible," not "can I answer any question I'd
like to ask of this treatment." The two are routinely conflated in
applied policy debate -- a well-identified headline effect gets treated
as license to speculate about mechanism, heterogeneity, or policy
interaction, when the data that would actually test those secondary
questions may not exist at a usable resolution, however good the
primary instrument is. This isn't a flaw in this analysis specifically;
it's a structural feature of most publicly available administrative
data, and arguably a quiet contributor to why policy debates about
mechanism (e.g. "does immigration policy offset labour market
disruption from X") often talk past each other -- the aggregate data
that would settle it doesn't exist at the level the argument is being
had.

**Follow-on work:** a second PhD project (using RAPID data) is planned
to pursue the granular version of this question that the current data
structurally cannot answer. This session's regional-level null results
are the direct motivation for why that more granular approach is
needed, not a competing or redundant effort.
